a small plane crash caused a near  disaster in northern New Jersey the  twin-engine plane plunged into a home in  Colonia yesterday about 20 miles  southwest of New York City  the pilot identified as dr. michael  schloss was killed but no one else was  hurt  mola legi is at the crash scene Lola  what are we hear from people close by  well good morning Anthony as you can see  behind me here by all of this the street  that the house sits on has been closed  off while authorities investigate the  crash scene the house is just beyond  this police barricade or what's left of  the house now neighbors in the area say  that they heard the plane sputtering and  then a loud bang just to give you an  idea of how devastating how much more  devastating this could have been we're  only about a quarter mile from a grammar  school that was in session at the time  of the crash this surveillance video  captured the moment the small  twin-engine Cessna plane nosedived just  before 11:00 a.m. this was the aftermath  a suburban New Jersey home on fire after  the plane crashed through the roof  tearing through two floors into the  basement I heard it hit and then you got  this loud like sonic boom and shook all  the houses it kind of looks like a  volcano erupted the plane took off from  Leesburg Virginia at around 9:50 Tuesday  morning it was supposed to land at  linden airport in new jersey before it  crashed about three and a half miles  away  the pilot was communicating with air  traffic control and was cleared for an  approach and the airplane subsequently  lost radar contact the family of three  who lived at the house luckily wasn't  there when the plane hit Mayor John  McCormick says the home was engulfed in  flames in just seconds everybody was  home I don't know if they would have had  a chance to escape so thank God now the  NTSB will spend the next few days  sorting through the rubble as we pull  that wreckage out piece by piece we'll  be examining the condition of those  components such as the flight controls  the engine everything that we can find  now the plane is expected to be removed  from the crash scene in the next two or  three days that could take a little  longer because of the poor weather in  the area  meanwhile the first preliminary report  on exactly  what happened why the plane went down  how it went down that's expected in  about ten days Tony so we should learn a  little more than very fortunate that  family wasn't home Ola thank you very  much
 

